@page "/docs/forms/rich-text-editors"

<Page>
    <DocsExample Title="RichTextEditor" ComponentType="typeof(RichTextEditor)">
        <Alert BackgroundColor="TablerColor.Info" Title="Needs the TabBlazor.TipTap package" class="mb-5">
            <div>
                <p class="mb-2">RichTextEditor and RichTextView are not in the TabBlazor package. Add the package and register it next to TabBlazor:</p>
                <ol class="mb-2">
                    <li><code>dotnet add package TabBlazor.TipTap</code></li>
                    <li><code>builder.Services.AddTabBlazorTipTap(o =&gt; o.AllowHtml(html =&gt; sanitizer.Sanitize(html)));</code></li>
                </ol>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The sanitizer removes unsafe markup before saved HTML is shown; RichTextView needs it, the editor itself works
                    without it. See Sanitizing HTML below for why and how to set it up. No script tag is needed:
                    the editor loads TipTap when it first renders. For markdown text with a preview, see
                    <a href="docs/forms/markdown-editors">Markdown Editor</a>, which is in TabBlazor itself.
                </p>
            </div>
        </Alert>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorBasic)" Title="Basic">
            <Description>A WYSIWYG editor built on TipTap, from the TabBlazor.TipTap package. The value is HTML and is sent to .NET when you pause typing (DebounceMs, 300 ms by default) or leave the editor. The toolbar, keyboard shortcuts and undo work in the browser without round trips. Alt+F10 moves to the toolbar and Escape goes back to the text. Ctrl+K edits the link at the caret.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorMinimal)" Title="Minimal toolbar">
            <Description>Toolbar picks the buttons and their order. RichTextToolbar.Minimal has the essentials (bold, italic, lists, link); list RichTextTool values for your own, with Separator between groups. The toolbar only picks buttons: what content is allowed stays with Extensions, so set both for a simple editor. Keyboard shortcuts such as Ctrl+U still work without a button, as long as the feature is on.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorAll)" Title="More features">
            <Description>Extensions turns on built-in features: tables (with a size picker and row and column tools while the caret is in a table), task lists, alignment, highlight and smart typography. Content of a feature that is off, such as a pasted table without Table, becomes plain paragraphs. MaxLength adds a character counter.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorUploads)" Title="Images and files">
            <Description>Set UploadFile to let users paste, drop or pick files. A placeholder appears at the caret and becomes an image or a link when your handler returns the URL; progress and cancel show under the editor. Images pasted from Word or a web page arrive as data URLs and are uploaded too, so nothing is stored inline as base64. This demo returns data URLs because the docs have no server.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorMentions)" Title="Mentions">
            <Description>With RichTextExtensions.Mention and MentionSearch, typing @@ shows matches from .NET. Arrow keys move, Enter or Tab picks, Escape closes. A mention is stored as a span with data-id, and RichTextMentions.GetIds reads the ids back, for example to notify people when you save.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorView)" Title="Showing saved content">
            <Description>RichTextView shows saved HTML with the editor's styles, through the sanitizer registered with AddTabBlazorTipTap(o =&gt; o.AllowHtml(...)). Without one it refuses to render, so stored content cannot run script in other users' browsers. Sanitize again where you save: a browser can post any HTML.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorSanitizer)" Title="Sanitizing HTML">
            <Description>
                <p>
                    The HTML value comes from the browser, and anyone can post any HTML to your app without using the editor. If you show
                    it to other users unchanged, a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, an <code>onclick</code> or a <code>javascript:</code> link in it runs
                    in their browsers (cross-site scripting). A sanitizer removes everything that is not on an allow-list of safe tags,
                    attributes, CSS properties and URL schemes. Edit the HTML on the left to see what survives.
                </p>
                <p class="mb-1">Content is filtered in three places, and only the last two protect you:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>In the editor.</strong> TipTap keeps only what its features know, and links only http, https, mailto and relative URLs. This keeps the editor clean, but it runs in the browser, so it is not a security check.</li>
                    <li><strong>Where you save.</strong> Sanitize the value in your API or service before storing it. This is your code: the component cannot do it, since the value reaches .NET as a plain bound string, and in WebAssembly it runs in the user's browser.</li>
                    <li><strong>Where you show it.</strong> RichTextView, and the read-only editor in static rendering, pass the HTML through the sanitizer you register. RichTextView throws without one, unless you set AllowUnsanitizedHtml because you sanitize when saving.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    TabBlazor.TipTap does not include a sanitizer, so WebAssembly apps that don't need one don't download it. These docs use the
                    <a href="https://github.com/mganss/HtmlSanitizer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HtmlSanitizer</a> package. Its defaults remove
                    the data attributes the editor writes, so allow those, or mentions lose their ids and task lists their check marks:
                </p>
                <pre class="mb-2">var sanitizer = new Ganss.Xss.HtmlSanitizer();
sanitizer.AllowedSchemes.Add("mailto");
foreach (var attribute in new[] { "data-type", "data-id", "data-label", "data-mention-suggestion-char", "data-checked", "rel" })
{
    sanitizer.AllowedAttributes.Add(attribute);   // mentions, task lists, link rel
}
sanitizer.AllowedTags.Add("input");               // task list check boxes
sanitizer.AllowedTags.Add("label");
sanitizer.AllowedAttributes.Add("type");
sanitizer.AllowedAttributes.Add("checked");
sanitizer.AllowedCssProperties.Clear();
sanitizer.AllowedCssProperties.Add("text-align"); // alignment, nothing else

builder.Services.AddTabBlazorTipTap(o =&gt; o.AllowHtml(html =&gt; sanitizer.Sanitize(html)));</pre>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    Use the same sanitizer where you save. The JSON value carries no markup to sanitize and is turned into safe HTML by RichTextJson, and the markdown value is shown
                    through an IMarkdownRenderer, which in TabBlazor.Markdown escapes raw HTML by default.
                </p>
            </Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorCustomExtension)" Title="Custom extensions and toolbar buttons">
            <Description>An app can add its own TipTap extensions: list the module in TipTapOptions.ExtensionModules, register the extension by name, and turn it on with CustomExtensions. The module imports TipTap from the editor's bundle so both share one ProseMirror. A ToolbarExtra button with data-rt-command runs a command in the browser; RunCommandAsync and InsertContentAsync do the same from .NET.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorJson)" Title="JSON value">
            <Description>Format="RichTextEditorFormat.Json" stores the TipTap document as JSON instead of HTML: lossless, and with no markup to sanitize. RichTextJson.ToHtml turns it into the editor's HTML in .NET, without a browser, so it also works in emails, PDFs and APIs; RichTextView shows it with Json instead of Html. The HTML is safe by construction: text is encoded, only known tags and attributes are written and unsafe links are dropped, so it needs no sanitizer. Nodes of your own extensions are left out unless you render them with TipTapOptions.Json.Nodes.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorMarkdown)" Title="Markdown value">
            <Description>Format="RichTextEditorFormat.Markdown" edits visually but stores markdown, with GitHub tables and task lists. Features markdown cannot store are turned off and leave the toolbar: underline, highlight and alignment. Mentions are stored as [@@ id="42" label="Anna"], which RichTextMentions.GetIds(value, RichTextEditorFormat.Markdown) reads. The markdown support is a separate 19 KB file, loaded only by editors that use it. Show saved markdown with an IMarkdownRenderer, such as the one from TabBlazor.Markdown.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
        <CodeSnippet Component="@typeof(RichTextEditorValidation)" Title="Validation">
            <Description>Inside a form the editor validates like any input. An empty editor is null, so [Required] works.</Description>
        </CodeSnippet>
    </DocsExample>
</Page>
